You are reverse-engineering a compiled C++ binary as a BLACK BOX. The binary is at /workspace/dataset/test15_executable.

Invocation contract: it takes EXACTLY 6 positional args: scheme host port path query fragment.
It prints 8 lines to stdout: scheme(), host(), port(), path(), query(), fragment(), to_string(), is_valid().

RULES OF ENGAGEMENT:
- You may ONLY run the binary and observe stdout/stderr/exit code. There is NO C++ source available; do not look for any.
- ALWAYS quote every argument in the shell, including empty ones: /workspace/dataset/test15_executable "http" "example.com" "80" "/" "" ""
- Make output unambiguous. Use a helper that numbers the lines and marks empties, e.g.:
    p(){ printf '### IN: [%s]\n' "$*"; /workspace/dataset/test15_executable "$@" >/tmp/o 2>/tmp/e; printf '  exit=%d\n' $?; nl -ba -s': ' /tmp/o | sed 's/$/<END>/'; if [ -s /tmp/e ]; then echo "  STDERR: $(cat /tmp/e)"; fi; }
  Then call p with 6 quoted args. Beware: trailing empty args are easy to lose — verify your helper really passes 6.
- Run MANY cases (30+ minimum). Batch them into a few Bash calls; do not do one call per case.
- Be exhaustive about the boundary cases in your assigned dimension.
- Do not guess. Every rule you report must be backed by an observed input/output pair you actually ran.

Baseline facts already established (re-verify if relevant to you):
- ("http","example.com","80","/","","")  -> lines: http / example.com / 80 / "/" / "" / "" / "http://example.com:80/" / 1
- ("http","example.com","-1","/","","") -> to_string is "http://example.com/"  (port omitted), port() prints -1
- ("http","example.com","0","/","","")  -> to_string is "http://example.com:0/"  (port 0 IS included)
- ("http","","80","/","","")            -> to_string is "http://:80/", is_valid=1
- ("","example.com","80","/","","")     -> to_string is "/" (scheme+authority both vanish), is_valid=1
- ("","","0","","","")                  -> to_string is "", is_valid=0

Report rules as precise, implementable statements (pseudocode-level), each with the concrete evidence line.


DIMENSION UNDER REVIEW: scheme-transform

A previous investigator probed this dimension and inferred the following rules. Your job is ADVERSARIAL: actively try to FALSIFY each rule by finding inputs where it predicts the wrong output. For every rule, design at least 2 new inputs (not already tested) that would expose the rule as wrong if it is wrong, run them, and compare prediction vs reality. Pay special attention to off-by-one boundaries, empty strings, sign boundaries, and whether a rule that was only tested with one other field set still holds when that field is empty.

RULES TO ATTACK:
[
  {
    "rule": "scheme is transformed by a pure per-byte ASCII lowercase map and nothing else: for each byte b in the input string, out_b = (0x41 <= b <= 0x5A) ? b + 0x20 : b. Exhaustive single-byte sweep over all 254 testable byte values (0x01-0xFF, excluding 0x0A which was tested separately) shows exactly bytes 0x41..0x5A are remapped to 0x61..0x7A and every other byte is identical.",
    "evidence": "Sweep of scheme=<single byte>, host=\"e\", port=\"80\", path=\"/\": MAPPED: 41->61 42->62 43->63 44->64 45->65 46->66 47->67 48->68 49->69 4a->6a 4b->6b 4c->6c 4d->6d 4e->6e 4f->6f 50->70 51->71 52->72 53->73 54->74 55->75 56->76 57->77 58->78 59->79 5a->7a  (all 228 other bytes unchanged). Spot checks: (\"HTTP\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line1 \"http\"; (\"HtTpS\",...) -> line1 \"https\"; (\"MAILTO\",...) -> line1 \"mailto\"; (\"ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ\",\"e\",\"1\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line1 hex 6162636465666768696a6b6c6d6e6f707172737475767778797a"
  },
  {
    "rule": "The transform is byte-identical to `LC_ALL=C tr 'A-Z' 'a-z'` for arbitrary multi-byte input: length-preserving, order-preserving, no dedup, no insertion/removal of any byte.",
    "evidence": "16-string corpus compared byte-for-byte against LC_ALL=C tr output, all OK, any_diff=0. e.g. in=4d695865442d436153652e3132332b58 (\"MiXeD-CaSe.123+X\") -> got=6d697865642d636173652e3132332b78 == exp_ascii_tolower; in=413a422f433f442345 (\"A:B/C?D#E\") -> got=613a622f633f642365"
  },
  {
    "rule": "NO whitespace trimming is performed. Leading and trailing spaces are preserved exactly (and are then emitted into to_string, producing a malformed URL).",
    "evidence": "(\" http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line1 \" http\", line7 \" http://example.com:80/\"; (\"http \",...) -> line1 \"http \", line7 \"http ://example.com:80/\"; (\"   HTTP   \",...) -> line1 \"   http   \", line7 \"   http   ://example.com:80/\"; (\"  \",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line1 \"  \", line7 \"  ://example.com:80/\", is_valid=1"
  },
  {
    "rule": "Internal whitespace is preserved (no whitespace removal/collapsing anywhere in the string).",
    "evidence": "(\"ht tp\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line1 \"ht tp\", line7 \"ht tp://example.com:80/\"; (\"HT TP\",...) -> line1 \"ht tp\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "All ASCII control characters are stored verbatim: TAB(0x09), LF(0x0A), CR(0x0D), VT(0x0B), FF(0x0C), 0x01, DEL(0x7F). They are neither stripped nor trimmed, and are re-emitted in to_string.",
    "evidence": "arg1 hex 0b 68 74 74 70 0c -> cat -A line1 \"^Khttp^L$\", line7 \"^Khttp^L://example.com:80/$\"; arg1 hex 01 48 54 54 50 7f -> line1 \"^Ahttp^?$\", line7 \"^Ahttp^?://example.com:80/$\"; arg1 hex 0d 48 54 54 50 0d -> line1 \"^Mhttp^M$\"; arg1 hex 48 54 0a 54 50 -> stdout lines 1-2 are \"ht\",\"tp\" and lines 8-9 are \"ht\",\"tp://example.com:80/\" (LF kept inside the stored scheme); arg1 hex 09 41 09 -> line1 hex 09 61 09"
  },
  {
    "rule": "No trailing separator stripping: a trailing ':' , '//' or '://' in the scheme argument is NOT removed. to_string then emits the stored scheme followed by its own literal \"://\", producing doubled separators.",
    "evidence": "(\"http:\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line1 \"http:\", line7 \"http:://example.com:80/\"; (\"http://\",...) -> line1 \"http://\", line7 \"http://://example.com:80/\"; (\"HTTP://\",...) -> line1 \"http://\", line7 \"http://://example.com:80/\"; (\"://\",...) -> line1 \"://\", line7 \"://://example.com:80/\"; (\":\",...) -> line1 \":\", line7 \":://example.com:80/\"; (\"//\",...) -> line1 \"//\", line7 \"//://example.com:80/\"; (\"http:/\",...) -> line1 \"http:/\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "No scheme syntax validation or rejection: schemes starting with a digit or non-alpha, and schemes containing '_', '-', '.', '+', '%', '@', '[', ']', '{', '}', '\\\\', '^', '`', '|', '~', ':' or '/' are all stored verbatim (modulo ASCII lowercase), and is_valid() is unaffected (still 1 for the standard host/path combo).",
    "evidence": "(\"1http\",...) -> line1 \"1http\", is_valid 1; (\"+http\",...) -> \"+http\"; (\"ht_tp\",...) -> \"ht_tp\"; (\"ht-tp\",...) -> \"ht-tp\"; (\"ht.tp\",...) -> \"ht.tp\"; (\"ht+tp\",...) -> \"ht+tp\"; (\"HTTP2\",...) -> \"http2\"; (\"SVN+SSH\",...) -> \"svn+ssh\"; (\"X-Y.Z+W\",...) -> \"x-y.z+w\"; (\"@[]{}\\\\^`|~\",...) -> \"@[]{}\\\\^`|~\", line7 \"@[]{}\\\\^`|~://example.com:80/\", is_valid 1; (\"%48%54%54%50\",...) -> \"%48%54%54%50\" (no percent-decoding); (\"ht:tp\",...) -> \"ht:tp\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "No percent-decoding and no percent-encoding is applied to the scheme.",
    "evidence": "(\"%48%54%54%50\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line1 \"%48%54%54%50\" (not \"HTTP\"/\"http\"), line7 \"%48%54%54%50://example.com:80/\"; (\"ht tp\",...) -> line1 \"ht tp\" (space not encoded as %20)"
  },
  {
    "rule": "Bytes >= 0x80 are passed through untouched: no UTF-8 aware case folding and no single-byte (Latin-1) case folding. UTF-8 multibyte sequences survive byte-exactly while ASCII letters in the same string are still lowered.",
    "evidence": "(\"htTPÜ\",...) arg hex 68 74 54 50 c3 9c -> line1 \"httpM-CM-^\\\" i.e. hex 68 74 74 70 c3 9c (0xC3 0x9C untouched); (\"日本\",...) arg hex e6 97 a5 e6 9c ac -> line1 identical hex; raw Latin-1 bytes arg hex dc c9 d6 -> line1 hex dc c9 d6 (0xDC 'Ü' NOT lowered to 0xFC); \"ÀÉÎÕÜ\" (c380c389c38ec395c39c) -> line1 identical; \"ΑΒΓ\" (ce91ce92ce93) -> identical; \"АБВ\" (d090d091d092) -> identical; \"Ǆǅǆİı\" (c784c785c786c4b0c4b1) -> identical; hex 48 54 54 50 ff fe -> line1 hex 68 74 74 70 ff fe"
  },
  {
    "rule": "The transform is locale-independent (equivalent to the C locale / a hand-rolled ASCII range check). Setting LC_ALL/LANG does not change which bytes are mapped.",
    "evidence": "Full-byte sweep repeated under LC_ALL/LANG = default, C, C.UTF-8, de_DE.iso88591, tr_TR.utf8, en_US.utf8 — every run reported the identical MAPPED set 41->61 ... 5a->7a and nothing else. Also LC_ALL=tr_TR.utf8 (\"HTTPI\",\"e\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line1 \"httpi\" (dotless-i mapping NOT applied)"
  },
  {
    "rule": "No length limit and no truncation; the stored scheme has exactly the same byte length as the input, up to at least 100000 bytes.",
    "evidence": "scheme = 'A' x N with host example.com, port 80, path \"/\": N=100 -> line1 length 100, all chars 'a', line7 length 118 ending \"://example.com:80/\"; N=500 -> len 500 / line7 518; N=1000 -> 1000 / 1018; N=5000 -> 5000 / 5018; N=100000 -> line1 length 100000, unique chars = {a}, line7 length 100018"
  },
  {
    "rule": "Empty scheme stays empty (no default like \"http\" is substituted), and to_string then omits both the scheme and the literal \"://\" separator entirely.",
    "evidence": "(\"\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line1 \"\" (empty), line7 \"/\" (host and port also vanish), is_valid 1. Contrast (\"HTTP\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 \"http://example.com:80/\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "to_string() embeds the ALREADY-NORMALIZED scheme, byte-identically: for every non-empty scheme tested, line7 == line1 + \"://\" + <rest>. The lowercasing is applied once at construction/storage time, not separately in to_string.",
    "evidence": "Automated prefix check over 27 non-empty schemes (host example.com, port 80, path \"/\") — all MATCH against expected line1+\"://example.com:80/\": HTTP->http, HtTpS->https, \"Http:\"->\"http:\", \"HTTP://\"->\"http://\", \"://\", \":\", \"//\", 1HTTP->1http, \"+HTTP\"->\"+http\", HT_TP->ht_tp, HT-TP, HT.TP, HT+TP, HTTP2->http2, \" HTTP \"->\" http \", \"HT TP\"->\"ht tp\", htTPÜ->httpÜ, 日本, %48TTP->%48ttp, \"@[]{}\", MAILTO->mailto, FILE->file, URN->urn, A->a, Z->z, AZ->az, aZ->az. The only reported MISMATCH was scheme=\"\" (see empty-scheme rule)."
  },
  {
    "rule": "The \"://\" separator is emitted by to_string whenever the stored scheme is non-empty, even if host is empty — so the scheme transform is visible in to_string independently of the authority.",
    "evidence": "(\"HTTP\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"\") -> line1 \"http\", line7 \"http://\", is_valid 0; (\"HTTP\",\"\",\"-1\",\"/p\",\"\",\"\") -> line7 \"http:///p\", is_valid 1; (\"HTTP\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"q\",\"\") -> line7 \"http://?q\"; (\"HTTP\",\"\",\"-1\",\"\",\"\",\"f\") -> line7 \"http://#f\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "The lowercasing is applied ONLY to the scheme component — host, path, query and fragment are left case-intact. So the transform is a scheme-specific normalization, not a global one.",
    "evidence": "(\"HTTP\",\"EXAMPLE.COM\",\"8080\",\"/PATH\",\"Q=1\",\"FRAG\") -> line1 \"http\" but line2 \"EXAMPLE.COM\", line4 \"/PATH\", line5 \"Q=1\", line6 \"FRAG\", line7 \"http://EXAMPLE.COM:8080/PATH?Q=1#FRAG\""
  },
  {
    "rule": "The transform is idempotent: feeding an already-lowercase scheme returns it unchanged.",
    "evidence": "(\"abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz\",\"e\",\"1\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line1 hex 6162...797a identical to input hex; (\"http\",\"example.com\",\"80\",\"/\",\"\",\"\") -> line1 \"http\""
  }
]

STATED UNCERTAINTIES (resolve these definitively by experiment):
[
  "Cannot distinguish the implementation idiom: `std::transform(s.begin(), s.end(), s.begin(), [](unsigned char c){ return std::tolower(c); })` (with a correct unsigned-char cast, C locale) is behaviorally IDENTICAL to a hand-rolled `if (c >= 'A' && c <= 'Z') c += 32;`. Both match every one of the 254 observed byte mappings. The binary evidently never calls setlocale(LC_ALL, \"\") / imbues a non-C locale, since 6 different LC_ALL/LANG settings produced identical maps — so the ambiguity is unresolvable by black-box observation.",
  "Embedded NUL bytes in the scheme cannot be tested: execve/argv terminates the string at the first 0x00, so $'HT\\x00TP' reached the program as just \"HT\". Whether from_components would preserve an interior NUL in a std::string is unobservable through this CLI.",
  "Byte 0x0A was excluded from the automated single-byte sweep because it splits stdout lines; it was verified manually instead (arg hex 48540a5450 -> scheme() emits \"ht\\ntp\", and to_string() re-emits the same \"ht\\ntp\" before \"://\"), so LF is preserved, but it was not covered by the mechanical hex-equality comparison.",
  "No upper bound on scheme length was found (100000 bytes stored intact); a limit may exist above that but was not probed, nor was allocation-failure behavior.",
  "This dimension only observed that is_valid() stays 1 for arbitrary garbage schemes when host+path are well-formed, and that scheme=\"\" collapses the whole scheme://authority prefix. Whether the *value* of a non-empty scheme feeds any other logic (e.g. default-port selection, scheme-specific validation, is_valid rules) was not exhaustively explored — that belongs to the port/validity dimensions. Note (\"http\",...,\"80\",...) keeps :80 in to_string, so no http/80 default-port suppression was seen.",
  "The `stoi` abort on non-numeric port (exit 134, std::invalid_argument) happens before any output, which means no scheme case can be tested with a non-numeric port argument. All scheme conclusions are therefore conditioned on a parseable integer port."
]

Then write finalSpec: complete, unambiguous pseudocode for this dimension that a Rust implementer can follow with zero guesswork. Include the exact literal outputs for any case where the behavior is surprising.

